[32;1m[1;3mQuestion: A factory plans purchasing and production for 100 products (P1–P100) over a single planning horizon. Each product can only be purchased/produced in whole batches, where one batch equals 10 units. So, batch_size_units is fixed batch size in units =10. Producing one unit of any product consumes three limited resources (R1, R2, R3). Each product also faces an upper demand bound; producing beyond this bound is not allowed (unmet demand is permitted). The goal is to choose batch quantities to maximize total profit (=sum over products of (10 * x_i * profit_per_unit_i)) while respecting all resource and demand limits.

    The referring data is listed in the csv file. factory_products_100.csv consists of profit earned per produced unit (positive, real). Among them r1_per_unit, r2_per_unit, r3_per_unit represent resource consumption per produced unit for R1, R2, R3 (real) respectively. upper_demand_units means maximum market demand (units) for the product (integers not equal to multiples of 10, so batch decisions meaningfully interact with demand).
    resources_capacities.csv includes total available amount of each resource in the horizon (real)

Thought: I need to identify the mathematical structure: The objective is to maximize total profit from producing/purchasing products. Decision variables are integer batch counts per product (each batch = 10 units). Constraints: (1) total resource consumption across all products cannot exceed available capacities for R1, R2, R3; (2) for each product, total produced units (10 * batch count) cannot exceed the product's upper demand bound (which is not a multiple of 10, so batch decisions interact with demand limits). The batch-size restriction means that production can only occur in discrete multiples of 10 units, and this interacts with the upper demand bound in a nontrivial way.

Action: FileQA

